<h1>Tameka Walton</h1>
<p>Chicago Board of Education, District 10a, on the Chicago ballot November 3, 2026. Declared write-in. Endorsed by the Chicago Teachers Union.</p>
<p>These names are not printed on the ballot. To vote for one, write the name in the blank write-in space for that office and fill in the oval (on a touchscreen, follow the write-in steps on screen). Votes count only for write-ins who filed a declaration. The Chicago Board of Elections does not post those names, so this list comes from the Cook County Clerk&#39;s list and campaign announcements; election judges at your polling place have the official one.</p>
<h2>Running on</h2>
<p>Declared write-in candidate; the name is not printed on the ballot. She says her priorities are equity between schools, supporting student mental health, and giving Local School Councils a stronger voice in board decisions. She was removed from the ballot in July after some of her petition signatures were ruled ineligible, leaving her below the 500-signature requirement. The Chicago Teachers Union endorsed her as a write-in candidate.</p>
<h2>Background</h2>
<p>Founder and executive director of Restored Hope, a Chicago nonprofit that supports women and girls who have experienced violence and trauma; longtime Local School Council member and state-appointed member of the Illinois Torture Inquiry and Relief Commission.</p>
